From 37a29bbe013f2796afcdd92f4257035dfe2f9f0c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 00:05:08 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] [DOSKUTSU] patch 0297 -- let the hand-rolled paths follow the
 centred box

Patch 0296 gave the ATI Mach64 a correct picture by centring the 320x240
logical screen inside the smallest surface its VBE will offer, but it
relaxed no dimension gate. Six sites in Renderer.cpp demanded a window
surface of exactly 320x240 and fell through to the SDL software renderer
otherwise, and 0296 fixed only drawTileFast. The round-2 logs show what
that costs on the one card in the fleet that is oversized:

  Mach64 512x384  slowpath reason block: windim=1787 of 1787
                  fastpath audit: taken=0 (idx8=0) skipped=1787
  ViRGE  320x240  fastpath audit: taken=2828 skipped=0 pct_x10=1000

Every drawSurface call on that card leaves the hand-rolled blitters, and
the requirement was never "exactly the logical screen" -- it was "at
least the logical screen, so a 1:1 blit fits".

The correctness half is colour-modulated text. drawSurfaceColorMod takes
its destination from _ensure_handrolled_surface, so on an oversized
surface it reaches the fallback that hands an INDEX8 texture to
SDL_SetTextureColorMod. SDL_CalculateBlit1 has no SDL_COPY_MODULATE_COLOR
case in any combination, so a colourkeyed glyph lands on SDL_Blit_Slow --
a per-pixel RGB round trip with a reverse palette lookup, in place of the
engine's precomputed byte LUT. That is the pink map-name text the
operator reports, and the counters make the diversion explicit: 13173
modulated draws in the fallback bucket against the ViRGE's zero.

Relaxed here: _try_fast_blit, _try_fast_blit_mirrored,
_try_fast_blit_alpha, and _ensure_handrolled_surface for
drawSurfaceColorMod, fillRect and clearScreen.

Routed rather than relaxed: drawLine and drawRect pass center_active=false.
That is a routing decision, not a narrowing. The dimension match in
_dos_surface_fits happens before the flag is consulted, so a card offering
320x240 still takes the direct write at both sites; only an oversized
surface falls through to the renderer, and 0296's viewport already
translates and clips renderer draws into the centred box. The draw lands
in the right place either way. It is preferable here because both bound
every write against the surface, so a direct-write version would need a
per-pixel or per-edge clip to the logical box, and getting that wrong
writes into the margin rather than failing visibly -- while the renderer
does the clipping correctly for free on primitives too rare to measure.
Nothing about these two sites is pending.

Three things this needed that the existing machinery did not give:

  - a shared clip-then-translate. Every primitive clamps against the
    destination SURFACE, which equals the logical screen only while the
    surface does; oversized, a sprite hanging off the logical right edge
    would spill into the cleared margin instead of being cut. 0296
    open-coded this for one caller. Eight hand-written copies is how the
    margin eventually gets a stray tile in it.
  - a mirrored variant. A column-reversed blit maps a destination-left
    clip onto the source RIGHT edge. Getting it backwards does not crash
    and does not touch the margin -- it silently draws the wrong slice of
    the sheet, which survives a smoke test.
  - clearScreen restricted to the box. Both branches wrote the whole
    surface, which oversized would repaint the margin with the map's
    background colour every frame, undoing 0296's one-shot clear and
    pushing bytes that flip() then intersects away. The native path keeps
    its single contiguous memset: 240 short memsets are measurably worse
    than one long one, and that is the path the banked matrix was
    measured on.

Coordinate discipline, since the failure mode is invisible:
_record_dirty_rect applies the centring offset itself AND clips to the
logical box before translating, so it is still fed untranslated rects at
every touched site. Feeding it translated input would truncate and
displace in one step, leaving a rect that looks plausible in a log while
the flush addresses the wrong region -- correct picture, wrong bytes
flushed, presenting as tearing rather than as visibly broken output.

A rect clipped wholly outside the logical screen returns true, not false.
_blit_indexed already early-returns on a zero-pixel clamp and reports
success, so the caller counts a taken fastpath and draws nothing.
Returning false would push those onto the slow path and move the very
counters this patch is measured by.

Every site is gated on _dos_center_active, so a card offering 320x240
takes the literal pre-patch path -- not an equivalent one. That matters
because the banked benchmark matrix was measured there. The oversized
+ killswitch case is rejected too: 0296's hint leaves the surface
oversized while centring is off, and accepting it would draw the game 1:1
in a corner, a third picture belonging to neither A/B arm.

Killswitch SDL_HINT_DOSKUTSU_FASTPATH_OVERSIZED=0 stands the fast paths
down while keeping the centring. Deliberately separate from 0296's hint,
so Round G compares two blit routes rather than two pictures.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT FIX. The expected performance gain is unknown. The
16bpp probe that suggested the indexed blit dominates was contaminated by
drawTileFast writing one byte per pixel into a two-byte surface (its own
follow-up), and the residual bucket it would have to beat is derived by
subtraction from buckets that leak -- dc_ms scaled 2.36x on a flat
drawcall count, which queue dispatch cannot do. This patch addresses the
text colour and an unmeasured share of the frame. It does not touch the
59-65% unattributed residual, the episodic present plateaus, the SDL/0116
VBLANK cap, or the colour question if it survives.

Verification, all from witnesses already in the binary: the
`center-oversized: flush rect=WxH@X,Y` line must still report logical
size at the centring origin; `fastpath audit ... taken=` must go non-zero
on an oversized surface while `slowpath reason ... windim=` falls to
zero; and the native-320x240 no-op gets 0296's own treatment rather than
being asserted from the gate.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>
